  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry through Wednesday, then expect increase of \nscattered afternoon/evening showers/storms beginning Thursday \nand continuing through the weekend.\n\nHigh pressure over the region to maintain dry conditions \nthrough Wednesday. Shallow layer of moisture remains trapped \nbeneath the subsidence inversion over western NC, so have \nmaintained some low level cloudiness there, otherwise mostly \nclear skies to be found over much of the region. Dewpoints in \nthe 50s feel nice, but these numbers are not going to persist. \nLooking at one more pleasant night with temperatures slipping \nupper 50s to lower 60s, then trending warmer and more humid for \nthe second half of the week as both dewpoints and temperature \nincrease. Temperatures will once again flirt with 90 deg F for \ndaytime highs...summer refusing to release its grip.\n\nThe upper ridge is currently centered over the TN valley. This \nfeature is forecast to retrograde farther west and become \ncentered over the Arl-La-Tex by the end of the week. This will \nallow a front over the Great Lakes to sag south over the Mid-\nAtlantic with increasing moisture leading to a daily chance for \nshowers and a few storms beginning Thursday and persisting \nthrough the upcoming weekend. Overall, severe threat remains low\ngiven lack of shear to support organized deep convection.",
